# The one root command the CI user on the public demo may run, and nothing else.
#
# Tailscale SSH cannot restrict a command — the tailnet policy's `ssh` rules
# carry `action`, `src`, `dst`, `users`, `checkPeriod`, `acceptEnv` and
# `srcPosture`, and there is no `cmd` field — so an SSH grant is a shell grant.
# `selvage` on that box has blanket passwordless sudo, so granting CI `selvage`
# would mean "GitHub can be root on production". Hence a user of its own, whose
# only privilege is this line.
#
# There is deliberately **no argument wildcard**, and the trailing `""` is what
# says so: `/usr/local/sbin/selvage-deploy ""` permits that one program and no
# argument at all, whereas the same line without the `""` would permit *any*
# argument, because an empty argument list in sudoers means "any" rather than
# "none". That distinction is the whole bound: without the `""` the argument
# list is the script's own problem, and a wildcard there is a root shell.
#
# It is also why the deploy request arrives on stdin, and why the script refuses
# its own arguments as well: the marker is what sudo enforces, and the script
# check is the second lock on the same door.
#
# The script is root-owned mode 0755 under `/usr/local/sbin` (root:root 0755), so
# the user it is granted to cannot change what it does.
#
# Installs to `/etc/sudoers.d/selvage-deploy`, root:root, mode 0440, and is read
# with `visudo -c -f` before it is put there.
deployci ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/selvage-deploy ""
